; The activation chance of every skill, with the two cooking skills' own number
; replaced when the switch file is there.
;
; Half of a feature a build can leave out -- the other half is the switch,
; cooking_skill_chance.jaf beside it, and feature.js in this folder is where the
; pair is named. That file is also where the two skill Ids and the reasoning
; about them live; this one is about where the hook goes.
;
; PlayerSkill@PerSkill::get is the accessor for the スキルデータ table's 確率
; column, and it has three callers in the whole game: SceneBattle@CheckSkillPer
; (FUNC 24907), which is the roll -- checkRandomPercent(per), i.e.
; RAND(100) <= per -- and two in SacrificeFinder, which only ever look at leader
; skills (行動タイプ 20). Patching the accessor rather than the roll means every
; reader sees one consistent number, and it is the roll that stays untouched.
;
; This is a .jam rather than a .jaf because the replacement has to pass the
; skill's own Id to the switch, and the Id is on `this`. alice-tools' .jaf
; compiler resolves neither `this` nor a struct's own members inside an
; override, and it will not reach a property through a ref argument either --
; overriding SceneBattle@CheckSkillPer(ref PlayerAction action) instead, which
; needs no `this` at all, stops at
;   error: Invalid struct member name: Skill
;       in: action.Skill
; because PlayerAction@Skill is a property and not a field. What a .jam can do
; is assemble a whole function and repoint the function table at it, which is
; what --jam is for. It adds no locals -- --jam rewrites code and not the FUNC
; section -- and this function needs none: the two values it hands the switch
; both come off the stack.
;
; The first nine instructions are SceneBattle's own PlayerSkill@PerSkill::get
; verbatim, from
;   alice ain dump -c game/ain/Rance10.v1.04.ain
; and the tail is this feature. Regenerate it the same way if the game version
; changes: 28274 and 28263 are indices into one build of one .ain and mean
; nothing in the next.
;
; PlayerSkill@PerSkill::get#1 (FUNC 28275) is the compiler's twin of this
; getter, reading the same column through PlayerSkill@Id::get#1. Nothing in the
; .ain calls it -- no `PUSH 28275` anywhere in the dump -- so it is left as it
; is rather than patched blind.
;
; Save this file as UTF-8 with no BOM. The assembler reads a byte order mark as
; an instruction and stops on line 1, and an editor that defaults .jam to
; UTF-8-with-BOM will quietly put one back.
;
;   PUSHSTRUCTPAGE / PUSH 28263 / CALLMETHOD 0   this skill's Id
;   CALLFUNC CookingSkillPercent                 (確率, Id) -> the number to use
FUNC 28274
	S_PUSH "スキルデータ"
	PUSHSTRUCTPAGE
	PUSH 28263
	CALLMETHOD 0
	S_PUSH "確率"
	PUSH 0
	PUSH 0
	CALLFUNC EX_IA2Int
	PUSHSTRUCTPAGE
	PUSH 28263
	CALLMETHOD 0
	CALLFUNC CookingSkillPercent
	RETURN
	PUSH 0
	RETURN
ENDFUNC PlayerSkill@PerSkill::get
